### Express Router Parameter Modifiers

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Express 5.0.0-beta.1 introduced new parameter modifiers for the router: `?`, `*`, and `+`. These allow for more flexible route matching, supporting optional, zero-or-more, and one-or-more segments respectively.

```JavaScript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Matches '/users' or '/users/123'
app.get('/users/:id?', (req, res) => {
  res.send(`User ID: ${req.params.id || 'none'}`);
});

// Matches '/files/a/b/c'
app.get('/files/*', (req, res) => {
  res.send(`Path: ${req.params[0]}`);
});

// Matches '/items/a' or '/items/a/b/c'
app.get('/items/:slug+', (req, res) => {
  res.send(`Slug: ${req.params.slug.join('/')}`);
});
```

--------------------------------

### Express Route Parameter Handling

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Describes how Express handles custom parameter callbacks in routing. It specifies that `router.param()` callbacks are invoked only when necessary, improving efficiency.

```APIDOC
router.param(name, callback)

  - Registers a callback for route parameters.
  - The callback is invoked only when a route with the specified parameter name is matched.
  - Parameters:
    - name: The name of the parameter.
    - callback: A function `function(req, res, next, value, name)` that is executed when the parameter is found.
      - req: The request object.
      - res: The response object.
      - next: The next middleware function.
      - value: The value of the parameter.
      - name: The name of the parameter.

  - Related:
    - `app.param(name, callback)`: Similar functionality for the application level.
```

--------------------------------

### Express.js API: req.params Persistence

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Fixes an issue where modified `req.params[name]` values from `app.param()` handlers were not persisting correctly. This ensures that parameter modifications made within `app.param()` are available throughout the request lifecycle.

```APIDOC
app.param(name, fn)

Fix: Persistence of modified `req.params[name]`.

Description:
  This correction addresses a regression where changes made to `req.params[name]` within a custom `app.param()` handler were not being retained. After this fix, any modifications or additions to `req.params` within these handlers will persist and be accessible in subsequent middleware and route handlers for the same request.

Example:
  app.param('userId', (req, res, next, id) => {
    req.params.userId = parseInt(id, 10); // Modify parameter
    next();
  });

  app.get('/users/:userId', (req, res) => {
    // req.params.userId will now be the parsed integer
    res.send(`User ID: ${req.params.userId}`);
  });
```

--------------------------------

### Express.js API: Router mergeParams Option

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Introduces the `mergeParams` option for the `Router` class, enabling the merging of `req.params` from parent routes into child route parameters. This simplifies parameter access in nested routing structures.

```APIDOC
new Router([options])

Adds a `mergeParams` option to the Router constructor.

Parameters:
  - options: An object containing configuration options.
    - mergeParams: If true, parameters from parent routers will be merged into `req.params` of the child router.

Example:
  const express = require('express');
  const app = express();

  const adminRouter = express.Router({ mergeParams: true });
  adminRouter.get('/:id', (req, res) => {
    // If parent route was '/users/:userId/admin/:id',
    // req.params will contain { userId: '...', id: '...' }
    res.send(`Admin ID: ${req.params.id}, User ID: ${req.params.userId}`);
  });

  app.use('/users/:userId/admin', adminRouter);
  app.listen(3000);
```

--------------------------------

### `mergeParams` controls whether parent route params are passed to sub-router

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/test/app.router.js

Without `mergeParams`, `req.params` is overwritten when entering a sub-router mounted via `app.use('/user/:user', router)`. With `{ mergeParams: true }`, parent params are merged into the child's `req.params`.

```javascript
    it('should overwrite existing req.params by default', function (done) {
      var app = express();
      var router = new express.Router();

      router.get('/:action', function (req, res) {
        res.send(req.params);
      });

      app.use('/user/:user', router);

      request(app)
        .get('/user/1/get')
        .expect(200, '{"action":"get"}', done);
    })

    it('should allow merging existing req.params', function (done) {
      var app = express();
      var router = new express.Router({ mergeParams: true });

      router.get('/:action', function (req, res) {
        var keys = Object.keys(req.params).sort();
        res.send(keys.map(function (k) { return [k, req.params[k]] }));
      });

      app.use('/user/:user', router);

      request(app)
        .get('/user/tj/get')
        .expect(200, '[["action","get"],["user","tj"]]', done);
    })
```

